# Scaled residual source preread

2026-10-06. Independent source/API prerequisite audit by `phase_source_reviewer_20261005` at committed HEAD `d3936f55b5571098a02f7480f39b7d1895c0e351`. Proposal only: no candidate theorem review, Lean verification, new SAU, Poincare/BL or main-result admission. Compiler not run. Fresh primary sources were opened before reading the committed route proposal. The source-dependency-audit workflow is reused.

The [PBPS v1 source](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#S2.SS2) assumes C2 V, 0<alpha<=beta, eta>0 and beta*eta<=1. Its unreflected conditional potential is V(x)+||x-y||²/(2eta), with curvature alpha+eta^-1 through beta+eta^-1; (2.11) uses Poincare constant eta/(1+alpha*eta). [Appendix C.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06905v1#A3.SS1), HTML1197-1234, instead uses the normalized reflected law with W_y(u)=V((y+u)/2)+||u-y||²/(8eta). Its curvature is divided by four. Its parameter-score is s_y(u)=-gradV((y+u)/2)/2-(y-u)/(4eta), and its directional variance bound is (eta^-1-alpha)²||a||²/[4(alpha+eta^-1)]. This score is centered inside covariance, not assumed centered itself. Compact-smooth density and gradient closedness are stated; D.6 invokes Bakry-Emery Poincare, and D.9 invokes BL for linear covariance. Neither anchor constructs an epsilon resolvent or an epsilon-zero limit. A positive normalizer and the applicable integrability/domain conditions must be proved for actual tests.

[SPHMC v1 Section4.1](https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1), HTML403-430, uses the unreflected RGO for normalized source curvature kappa^-1 I<=HessV<=I and 0<eta<=1. Equation4.1 subtracts eta^-2 Cov from eta^-1 I. The BL covariance upper bound (kappa^-1+eta^-1)^-1 I supplies the lower smoothed Hessian (kappa+eta)^-1 I. CR supplies the other Hessian side. Its BL result is cited background; the residual construction below is an authored analytic prerequisite, not its printed proof.

The independent seven-slot contract for the proposed bounded edge is:

| Slot | Exact proposed object/boundary |
|---|---|
| Objects | Real complete Hilbert H,K; one closed partial-linear A:H→K, with genuine graph/domain. In the actual application A=D.closure on scalar/vector weighted L2 of one normalized Gibbs law. |
| Domains | The SAME original compact-smooth graph D and its closure; r_n=epsilon_n*u_n is produced in its linear domain. Ordinary H×K has max norm; use WithLp2 for orthogonal Hilbert graph arguments. |
| Quantifiers | Fix A and f; for every positive epsilon sequence tending to0, take actual variational u_n in the SAME domain. Conditional order is original common J/R/S→all y→one D_y→epsilon/f; fiberwise only, no jointly measurable choices. |
| Assumptions | Complete H,K, separable H for this sequential WeakDual route, actual closed graph; true variational equations; f orthogonal to EVERY kernel element. Actual Gibbs adapter requires probability/positiveZ, genuine L2 classes and mean integrability. No input Poincare, uniform ||u_n||, spatial moment or output limit certificate. |
| Conclusions | Strong H-norm convergence epsilon_n*u_n→0, with actual A(epsilon_n*u_n)→0. No claim of unscaled u convergence, epsilon0 solution/range, adjoint/core identity, BL or source theorem completion. |
| Scope | Abstract prerequisite followed by SAME-D scalar/centering adapters; later source-specific score and linear-observable consumers. Finite-dimensional Borel source E, including0D, supplies countably generated measure structure, not finite-dimensional L2. |
| Constants | Scaled residual estimates have constant1; no curvature m needed for that edge. Later scalar integration requires m>0. Reflected m=(alpha+eta^-1)/4; unreflected m=alpha+eta^-1. Do not transport constants by naming kernels alike. |

The [committed route](Scaled_residual_orthogonal_graph_route.md) is mathematically coherent under these hypotheses. Testing the real variational equation at u_n gives epsilon_n||u_n||²+||Au_n||²=<f,u_n>. Domain linearity and r_n=epsilon_n*u_n yield

    ||r_n||² + epsilon_n||Au_n||² = <f,r_n>,
    ||r_n|| <= ||f||,    ||Ar_n||² <= epsilon_n||f||².

These are derived estimates, not extra premises. Along any actual weakly convergent subsequence r_n⇀v, test (r_n,Ar_n) against each (a,b) in the orthogonal complement of the SAME graph, in WithLp2. The limit gives <v,a>=0, hence (v,0) belongs to the double orthogonal and therefore the closed graph. Thus v is in kerA. True kernel orthogonality gives <f,v>=0; the energy inequality then forces ||r_n||→0 along that subsequence without needing lower semicontinuity of norm. Apply this construction to every subsequence to obtain full-sequence convergence. Weak compactness alone does not identify this limit.

Read-only API evidence: `ClosedGraphResolvent.weak_resolvent` actually constructs variational u and energy on any closed partial-linear A, without dense domain. `GlobalWeightedResolventCoercivity.global_weak_resolvent_coercivity`, lines464-502, provides the genuine SAME-D variational equation plus m||Du||²<=||f-epsilon*u||²<=||f||² under explicit C2, BOTH Hessian bounds, positiveZ and exact tilted law. The new residual edge would consume these outputs, not assume them as a final residual certificate.

Pinned Mathlib candidates were checked in source: `WeakDual.isSeqCompact_closedBall` at Analysis/Normed/Module/WeakDual.lean370 inherits separable normed H and ProperSpace scalar; real scalars qualify. `InnerProductSpace.toDual`135 and `toDual_symm_apply`177 provide actual Riesz inverse/evaluation; the weak-dual eval criterion is Topology/Algebra/Module/Spaces/WeakDual.lean283. `WithLp.prod_continuous_ofLp`481 pulls back actual graph closedness; `WithLp.prod_inner_apply`47 gives the sum pairing. `Submodule.orthogonal_orthogonal_eq_closure`87 requires complete product space. Measure separability382 needs CountablyGenerated E and SFinite mu; Lp second-countability427 also needs separable coefficient space and 1<=p<infinity. Their actual instance chain and Riesz/subsequence/graph glue remain uncompiled adapters. No ignored prototype evidence is used.

A proposed route in at most seven steps:

1. Produce r_n in the same original closed domain and derive both scaled-energy estimates, handling f=0 without division by ||f||.
2. Derive actual scalar-L2 separability from finite-dimensional Borel E and actual finite conditional probability, with the pinned measure/Lp instances.
3. For every bounded subsequence, extract a genuine WeakDual subsequence of its Riesz image and pull its actual limit back to H; verify all inner-product evaluations.
4. Prove weak-plus-strong SAME-graph admission using the true WithLp2 graph and double orthogonal; conclude the weak limit belongs to kerA.
5. Combine actual forcing/kernel orthogonality with energy to force norm-zero on that subsequence, then whole-sequence norm convergence by contradiction.
6. Build the actual Gibbs/source centering adapter: L2 implies L1 for probability mu; weighted AE constants give <f,c>=c*integral(f). Mean-zero f is therefore kernel-orthogonal using the actual zero-gradient→constant result. Constant-domain membership permits domain subtraction and preserves Dz; no centering assumption on resolvent u is needed.
7. A SEPARATE subsequent integration may take centered original-domain z as forcing f=z. Its variational equation gives ||z||²=<r_n,z>+<Du_n,Dz>. Residual-zero and the existing bound ||Du_n||<=||z||/sqrt(m) yield m||z||²<=||Dz||², including z=0. Variance/score and linear-covariance consumers then need exact AE/L2 identifications and actual moments; they are not part of the proposed residual theorem.

The source adapter is a genuine missing edge: `ConditionalScoreClosedDomain.conditional_score_closed_gradient_domain` produces the actual noncompact score/gradient classes in the original D_y, so a future centered-domain inequality can apply to this score after subtracting its actual mean. Probability mass1, positive density/partition and SAME-D kernel identities must be used, not substituted by an auxiliary conditional kernel. Reflection x↦2x-y changes volume by Jacobian2^-d and gradient/Dirichlet scaling by four; S_y is its actual pushforward. An unreflected RGO covariance consumer must instantiate its own genuine law/curvature/moments. No R2=R, S/R law identity, measurable fiber selector or normalization shortcut is justified by this proposal.

Zero-dimensional E has one-point actual probability, zero gradient, all scalar L2 classes constant, and kernel-orthogonal forcing0. Generic real Hilbert H or K may also be trivial: Riesz/graph arguments and norm inequalities must avoid Nontrivial assumptions or unit-vector divisions. Arbitrary non-countably-generated probability spaces do not inherit the proposed sequential separability route. If future code adds separability to an abstract Hilbert theorem, it must be explicit; actual finite-dimensional Borel source applicability remains intact through a proved adapter.

